<Guide slug="how-to-find-your-zip-code" description="Five fast ways to find your ZIP code: detect it from your location, check your mail, search your city, use USPS lookup, or browse by state." faqs={faqs}>
  <h1>How to find your ZIP code</h1>
  <p class="lead" data-answer>The fastest way to find your ZIP code is to use <a href="/what-is-my-zip-code/">What’s my ZIP code?</a>, which detects it from your location in one tap. You can also check your mail, search your city, or look up your exact address with USPS.</p>

  <h2>1. Detect it from your location</h2>
  <p>Open <a href="/what-is-my-zip-code/">What’s my ZIP code?</a> and allow location access. The page matches your position to the nearest ZIP code on your device — nothing is uploaded.</p>

  <h2>2. Check your mail</h2>
  <p>Your ZIP code is the five-digit number at the end of the last line of your address, e.g. <code>AUSTIN TX 78701</code>. If you see nine digits (<code>78701-1234</code>), the first five are your ZIP code and the last four are your <a href="/guides/what-is-zip-plus-4/">ZIP+4</a>.</p>

  <h2>3. Search your city</h2>
  <p>Type your city into the <a href="/search/">ZIP code lookup</a>. Small towns usually have a single ZIP code; big cities list all of theirs on one page with a map, so you can spot your neighborhood.</p>

  <h2>4. Use the USPS lookup for an exact address</h2>
  <p>For official address validation — or to get ZIP+4 — use the <a href="https://tools.usps.com/zip-code-lookup.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">USPS ZIP code lookup</a>. It is the authoritative source for deliverable addresses.</p>

  <h2>5. Browse by state</h2>
  <p>Know the state but not the town? <a href="/states/">Browse ZIP codes by state</a>, then drill down by county or city.</p>

  <h2>Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Big cities have many ZIP codes.</strong> Houston and New York each have more than 100; Los Angeles has over 80.</li>
    <li><strong>ZIP codes cross city lines.</strong> Your ZIP may carry a neighboring town’s name.</li>
    <li><strong>PO Boxes have their own ZIP.</strong> It can differ from your home ZIP code.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
